---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

// Published 2026-11-10 on Jamey's go-ahead, with Attune 1.1.0 submitted for review on automatic
// release, so the page is live before the app that makes it true. Open items: attune/docs/privacy-1.1.0.md.
const updated = 'November 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Warren Labs"
  description="How Warren Labs and the Attune app handle your data. We never collect anything without asking, and we never collect who you are or what you listen to."
>
  <Nav variant="home" />
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h1 class="as-h2">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        <b>We never collect anything without asking, and we never collect who you are or what you
        listen to.</b> The Attune app contacts our server only if you press its Request this headphone
        button. That request carries the headphone name you typed, the app version and your platform,
        plus the network details every internet request carries, described below. This website
        stores an email address only if you choose to give us one.
      </p>

      <h3>What we will never collect</h3>
      <p>
        These are not settings you can turn on. We do not collect them and do not intend to build the
        ability to: your identity, your contact details, your listening history, the contents of your
        music library, your file paths, or anything else that could be tied back to you as a person.
        A tuning you make is yours; what you play through it is not our business.
      </p>

      <h3>Proof (iPhone, iPad and Mac)</h3>
      {/* CORRECTED 2026-09-18. This paragraph used to open "Proof makes no network connections."
          That absolute cannot survive the product it describes: Proof sells subscriptions, and an
          app that sells a subscription asks the App Store for its prices and for the purchase
          itself. The same sentence had already been retracted once inside the app, in
          proof/resources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy, whose header comment records that "There is no
          network code in the binary" was false and why — `nm -u` on the shipping binary lists
          NSURLSession, _socket and _connect, all pulled in by JUCE whether or not anything calls
          them. What survives is the whole privacy-load-bearing half, and it stays true after the
          store lands: no account, no server of ours, no analytics, no crash reporting, no
          telemetry, and nothing measured leaving the device. DO NOT RESTORE THE ABSOLUTE.
          The long form is at /proof/terms/, which has to keep agreeing with this. */}
      <p>
        <b>Proof has no account, no analytics, no crash reporting and no telemetry</b>, and nothing
        you measure leaves your device unless you export it yourself. We operate no service for it
        to send anything to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Buying Proof, restoring a purchase, or seeing its prices goes through the <b>App Store</b>.
        That connection is Apple's, on Apple's terms, and the transaction is between you and Apple.
        Nothing about your measurements is in it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Proof asks for the <b>microphone</b> permission, and it needs it for an ordinary reason: on
        iOS every audio input arrives through that one permission, including a USB or Thunderbolt
        audio interface. Proof reads the input while a measurement is running and while a live tool
        is on screen, and it does so to measure the signal. Nothing is recorded for any other
        purpose, nothing is transmitted, and nothing is retained beyond the measurement record you
        asked for.
      </p>
      <p>
        A measurement record is a readable text file containing the audio measurements themselves:
        curves, levels and the identity of the interface they were taken on. It is written to the
        app's own documents folder on your device. It is yours: you can read it, verify it, delete
        it, or export it, and we never receive a copy.
      </p>

      {/* Attune 1.1.0. Every fact below is checked in attune/docs/privacy-1.1.0.md against the code
          that makes it true: the app's one request (attune Source/WebViewShell.cpp,
          nativeRequestHeadphone) and what the server keeps (warren-labs tools/delivery,
          headphone-request.mjs and main.js). Change the code and that file first, then this page.
          App Privacy label (decided 2026-11-10): Data Not Linked to You, User Content and Other Data,
          purpose Analytics, no tracking. This section avoids the word "analytics" for Attune on
          purpose; it names exactly what is sent instead. */}
      <h3>Attune (Mac, iPhone and iPad)</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Attune has no account, no sign-in, no tracking and no crash reporting of its own. Nothing in
        it reports how you use it, and it sends us nothing unless you press the one button described
        below.</b> It runs on your
        device. Audio files you choose are read locally only to play them. Your music library, your
        file paths and your listening never leave your device, and your tunings leave only if you
        export or send them yourself.
      </p>

      <h3>Request this headphone</h3>
      <p>
        If we have not measured your headphone, you can type its name and press <b>Request this
        headphone</b>. <b>Only when you press it</b>, Attune sends our server three things: the name
        you typed, the app version, and whether you are on a Mac or on iOS. Nothing is sent as you
        type, nothing is sent in the background, and if you never press it, nothing is sent at all.
        There is no email address, no account and no device identifier in it. Please type only the
        headphone's name, because we keep what you type.
      </p>
      {/* Deno Deploy checked 2026-11-10 (attune/docs/privacy-1.1.0.md item 2): its traces keep only
          method, status and URL, for one day, and no client IP, so "we do not tie requests to you"
          holds. If that changes, say so here. Retention (Jamey, 2026-11-11): 30 days, implemented in
          warren-labs bb6fc8d (tools/delivery/headphone-request.mjs). Each model record lives 30 days
          from its first request and day totals are deleted after 30 days, swept daily. The next
          request after a record's window starts a fresh record and emails again (countModel), so the
          page says so: "the first request" alone under-stated what reaches the mailbox. This page must
          not go live before that server change is deployed. The email stays in our mailbox until
          deleted; Resend keeps its copy 30 days (all self-serve plans, resend.com/pricing, checked
          2026-11-11). Change the code first, then this page. */}
      <p>
        For each model we keep the name as it was first typed, how many times it has been requested,
        and the dates of the first and latest request, for <b>30 days from the first request</b>. We
        also keep a total count for each day, and delete each day's count after 30 days. A request
        after those 30 days starts a new record. The first request for a model, and the first of each
        new record, is also emailed to us with its app version and platform, through
        <b>Resend</b>. That email stays in our mailbox until we delete it, and Resend keeps its copy
        for 30 days. The server runs
        on <b>Deno Deploy</b>. Like any request over the internet, it
        also carries your IP address, so that the answer can reach you, and the standard details your
        device adds to every web request (the app's name and build, the system version and your
        language setting). Our code does not read or store any of that, and we do not tie requests to
        you. That also means we cannot find your request afterward, or reply to it. We use the counts
        to decide what to measure next.
      </p>

      <h3>Apple's parts</h3>
      <p>
        Buying Attune Pro, restoring it, or seeing its price goes through the <b>App Store</b>. Attune
        may also show Apple's standard prompt to rate the app. Both are Apple's, on Apple's terms, and
        what you do in them goes to Apple, not through us. Your music and your tunings are not in it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Apple also shows us figures it collects itself: App Store numbers such as how often Attune's
        page is viewed and how often it is downloaded and, from people who have turned on sharing with
        app developers in their device's analytics settings, crash reports and usage totals. That is
        Apple's setting and Apple's data, collected by Apple, not by anything in Attune, and none of
        it names you to us.
      </p>

      <h3>Feedback, through your own email</h3>
      <p>
        Something you send yourself, and it is always optional. If you use <b>Have feedback?</b>, or
        Attune invites you to write to us, the app writes a message and opens it in <em>your</em> mail
        app, with the technical context it attaches (app version, operating system version, output
        device, which headphone pair is loaded) shown to you in the window before you send it. It goes
        through your own email account, so we see your address and what you wrote, as with any email
        you send us. You send it; Attune doesn't.
      </p>

      <h3>If that ever changes</h3>
      <p>
        We may one day want crash reports, so bugs get fixed without someone having to write in, or
        anonymous results from the listening exam, so the house target improves for everyone. If we
        build either, all of the following are binding: it is <b>off by default</b>; we ask once, in
        plain language, and say what it's for; <b>you can see the exact payload</b> before you agree
        and any time after; one click turns it off permanently; and <b>this page is updated first</b>,
        before the feature ships, never after. Still nothing from the list above, and still nothing
        tied to you. A pre-ticked box, a consent bundled into something else, or a friendly dialog
        that defaults to yes would all break that promise, so we won't use them.
      </p>

      <h3>This website (warrenlabs.com)</h3>
      <p>
        If you enter your email address in a signup, waitlist, measurement-request or feedback form,
        we use it only to reply and to contact you about Warren Labs. It is stored by our form
        provider (Formspree) and by us, and is never sold or shared with advertisers. Ask us to
        delete it at any time. The site sets no advertising or cross-site tracking cookies. The
        feedback form attaches the page you were on, your window size and your browser version,
        shown to you before you send, and email is optional there.
      </p>
      {/* Plausible (2026-11-10): Jamey: still on, may pay later; keep this paragraph. If Plausible
          stops counting visits, "We count visits with Plausible" becomes false: change it then. */}
      <p>
        We count visits with <b>Plausible</b>, and it is worth saying exactly what that does and
        does not do. It records the page, the referring site, rough country, and device type. It
        sets <b>no cookies</b>, stores nothing on your device, assigns you no identifier, and cannot
        follow you to any other website. There is no profile of you, here or anywhere, and nothing
        it collects is personal data, which is why this site has never asked you to accept a cookie
        banner and still doesn&rsquo;t. Aggregate numbers are visible to us only. If you would
        rather not be counted at all, any tracker blocker will stop it, and nothing on the site
        breaks if you do.
      </p>

      <h3>If you buy something</h3>
      <p>
        <b>FastSpring is the merchant of record</b> for purchases, meaning the legal seller. You enter your
        payment details on their checkout, not on ours. <b>We never see or store your card number</b>,
        and there is no version of our systems where we could: it never reaches us. FastSpring keeps
        its own records as the seller, under its own privacy policy.
      </p>
      <p>
        What reaches us is your <b>name, email address, and what you bought</b>. We need the first
        two to issue your licence and to answer you if something breaks, and the third to know which
        product you're asking about. We don't build a profile from it, we don't enrich it with data
        bought from anyone, and it is never sold or shared with advertisers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your licence file carries your name and email inside it, signed. That is what makes it yours
        rather than a code anyone could pass around. It lives on your machine and is never uploaded.
        The plugin verifies it offline and has no network access at all.
      </p>

      <h3>Emails about software you bought</h3>
      <p>
        Licences, download links and update notices are sent through <b>Resend</b>, our email
        provider. If you buy a product, you will get emails about <em>that</em> product: the
        licence itself, and a note when it updates. That is part of buying it, and we tell you so at
        the point of purchase rather than burying it here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hearing about <em>other</em> Warren Labs tools is a <b>separate, unticked box</b> you have to
        choose. We don't pre-tick it, we don't bundle it into the purchase, and not ticking it costs
        you nothing. Every email we send has a real one-click unsubscribe that works immediately.
      </p>

      <h3>The measurement service</h3>
      <p>
        If you send a headphone in, we need your name and address to ship it back, and we keep them
        for that. Published measurements carry a credit line only in the form you choose: your name,
        a handle, or nothing at all.
      </p>

      <h3>Why it's written this way</h3>
      <p>
        Attune earns nothing from what you decide to buy, so the app has no reason to want a profile
        of you. Not collecting it isn't a sacrifice; it's just what this product doesn't need.
      </p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p>
        Questions, or a deletion request: <a href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com">jamey@warrenlabs.com</a>.
        Warren Labs is a trade name of <b>Free River Studios LLC</b>, Livingston, Montana, which is
        the data controller for anything described on this page.
      </p>

      <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer variant="home" />
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose h3 { margin: 30px 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .prose p { margin: 0 0 14px; line-height: 1.65; }
  .prose .updated { margin-top: 30px; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.55; }
</style>
